"""Additional daily topics that deserve their own lessons."""
def register(lesson):
    lesson(0,'formatting','Formatting, comments, and documenting intent',
    'A program communicates with both the computer and the people maintaining it. Formatting output converts values into a representation for a person or another program. Comments explain intent that the code alone cannot express. Documentation comments describe a public contract and can contain examples that Cargo tests.',
    '/// Formats a human-facing label for a known count.\nfn label(count: usize) -> String { format!("Items: {count:03}") }\nfn main() {\n    assert_eq!(label(7), "Items: 007");\n    println!("{:?}", [1, 2, 3]);\n}',
    'format! constructs a String instead of printing. The width specifier pads the count with zeros. Debug formatting displays the array for inspection. The triple-slash comment documents the function rather than changing its execution.',
    'A CLI prints readable status while a library returns values and documents how callers should interpret them.',
    'Debug output is not a stable serialization format. Avoid relying on it as a stored protocol.',
    'Why should a comment explain a decision rather than repeat the next line?',
    'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/fmt/')
    lesson(1,'conversions','Operators, numeric conversions, and explicit units',
    'Rust generally avoids silently converting unrelated numeric types. A conversion can lose range, precision, or meaning. Use TryFrom when values may not fit, and distinguish parsing text from converting an already numeric value. Operators have type-specific behavior, so integer division and floating-point division implement different contracts.',
    'fn main() {\n    let raw: u16 = 300;\n    assert!(u8::try_from(raw).is_err());\n    assert_eq!(i64::from(12u8), 12);\n    assert_eq!(7 / 2, 3);\n    assert_eq!(7 % 2, 1);\n    let enabled = 0b1010u8 & 0b0010 != 0;\n    assert!(enabled);\n}',
    'A narrowing conversion is rejected when 300 cannot fit in a byte. The widening conversion is infallible. Quotient and remainder describe integer division, and bitwise AND tests a protocol flag.',
    'A binary message parser converts lengths and flags into validated application values.',
    'A cast that compiles may truncate or change the interpretation of a value.',
    'When is wrapping arithmetic an intended rule rather than a bug?',
    'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/convert/')
    lesson(3,'patterns','Patterns, destructuring, guards, and exhaustiveness',
    'Patterns inspect the structure of a value and bind pieces of it. They appear in match arms, let bindings, function parameters, and loops. Refutable patterns can fail to match and need a control-flow construct that handles failure. Irrefutable patterns always match the type being bound.',
    'fn classify(point: (i32, i32)) -> &\'static str {\n    match point {\n        (0, 0) => "origin",\n        (x, y) if x == y => "diagonal",\n        (_, 0) => "horizontal axis",\n        _ => "other",\n    }\n}\nfn main() { assert_eq!(classify((3, 3)), "diagonal"); assert_eq!(classify((0, 0)), "origin"); }',
    'Arms are considered in order. The origin arm precedes the guarded diagonal arm because the origin satisfies both conditions. Underscore ignores a component; the final wildcard ensures every remaining pair has a result.',
    'A protocol handler classifies structured messages while keeping field extraction next to the relevant branch.',
    'A broad earlier arm can make a later specific case unreachable or semantically irrelevant.',
    'How do a wildcard and a named underscore-prefixed binding differ?',
    'ch19-03-pattern-syntax.html')
    lesson(4,'collection-design','Dequeues, heaps, and choosing a collection',
    'A collection is a promise about storage, operations, and ordering. VecDeque supports efficient insertion and removal at both ends. BinaryHeap exposes the largest item first by default. Reverse changes ordering so the smallest item becomes the next one returned. Choose according to the operations your algorithm performs repeatedly.',
    'use std::{collections::{VecDeque, BinaryHeap}, cmp::Reverse};\nfn main() {\n    let mut queue = VecDeque::from(["first", "second"]);\n    assert_eq!(queue.pop_front(), Some("first"));\n    queue.push_back("third");\n    let mut deadlines = BinaryHeap::from([Reverse(30), Reverse(10), Reverse(20)]);\n    assert_eq!(deadlines.pop(), Some(Reverse(10)));\n}',
    'The queue preserves first-in-first-out order. The heap prioritizes the smallest deadline using Reverse; it does not promise that arbitrary iteration is fully sorted.',
    'A scheduler uses a queue for ready work and a priority structure for future deadlines.',
    'A heap efficiently selects the next priority but is not a substitute for a sorted sequence in every operation.',
    'How would you make equal-priority jobs run in a deterministic order?',
    'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/')
    lesson(6,'custom-iterator','Implement Iterator and expose a reusable sequence',
    'Implementing Iterator lets a custom state machine work with for loops and standard adapters. The next method mutates internal state and returns an optional item. Returning None indicates no item for that call; stronger guarantees such as never yielding again after exhaustion require the appropriate implementation and contract.',
    'struct Countdown(u8);\nimpl Iterator for Countdown {\n    type Item = u8;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u8> {\n        if self.0 == 0 { None } else { let n = self.0; self.0 -= 1; Some(n) }\n    }\n}\nfn main() { assert_eq!(Countdown(3).collect::<Vec<_>>(), [3, 2, 1]); }',
    'Each call returns the current positive count and advances the state. collect repeatedly requests values until exhaustion. The associated Item type tells consumers what each step yields.',
    'A record reader can expose logical records through standard iterator combinators instead of a bespoke traversal API.',
    'Do not claim an exact length or fused behavior unless every possible state satisfies the promise.',
    'What should happen when next is called repeatedly after this iterator reaches zero?',
    'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html')
    lesson(8,'build-config','Build scripts, conditional compilation, and compatibility',
    'Conditional compilation selects code at build time. cfg can include or exclude an item, whereas cfg! produces a boolean expression. Cargo build scripts can discover native libraries or generate files, but become part of the build trust and reproducibility boundary. Document supported targets and compiler versions explicitly.',
    '#[cfg(target_pointer_width = "64")]\nfn pointer_bytes() -> usize { 8 }\n#[cfg(not(target_pointer_width = "64"))]\nfn pointer_bytes() -> usize { std::mem::size_of::<usize>() }\nfn main() { assert_eq!(pointer_bytes(), std::mem::size_of::<usize>()); }',
    'Only the matching function definition is compiled. The assertion checks the platform representation instead of assuming every machine uses the same pointer width.',
    'A crate provides platform adapters while keeping its public behavior consistent across operating systems.',
    'An ordinary if cfg!(...) does not prevent the other branch from being type-checked.',
    'Why must a build script declare the inputs that should cause it to rerun?',
    'https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/build-scripts.html')
    lesson(10,'network-framing','TCP streams, message framing, and partial I/O',
    'TCP carries an ordered byte stream rather than preserving application message boundaries. A read may return part of a message or several messages together. A protocol needs framing, such as a delimiter or a length prefix, and must handle incomplete input without losing parser state.',
    'fn frame(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Option<&[u8]>, &\'static str> {\n    if bytes.len() < 2 { return Ok(None); }\n    let n = u16::from_be_bytes([bytes[0], bytes[1]]) as usize;\n    if n > 1024 { return Err("frame too large"); }\n    Ok(bytes.get(2..2 + n))\n}\nfn main() { assert_eq!(frame(&[0, 3, 65]), Ok(None)); assert_eq!(frame(&[0, 1, 65]), Ok(Some(&[65][..]))); }',
    'The two-byte big-endian length is checked before selecting a payload. None means more bytes are needed. A real streaming decoder must also consume the completed frame and preserve trailing bytes for the next frame.',
    'A telemetry connection receives sensor records split across arbitrary network reads.',
    'A single successful read is not proof that a complete logical message arrived.',
    'How should the decoder treat EOF halfway through a declared payload?',
    'https://tokio.rs/tokio/tutorial/framing')
    lesson(11,'typestate','Typestate, builders, and API design',
    'A type can encode which operations are available at a particular lifecycle stage. A transition consumes the old state and returns a new type, making reuse of the previous owned state impossible in safe code. Builders organize construction when many configuration fields are involved, but still need a clear validation contract.',
    'struct Draft { body: String }\nstruct Published { body: String }\nimpl Draft {\n    fn publish(self) -> Result<Published, Self> {\n        if self.body.is_empty() { Err(self) } else { Ok(Published { body: self.body }) }\n    }\n}\nfn main() {\n    let draft = Draft { body: "note".into() };\n    match draft.publish() { Ok(post) => assert_eq!(post.body, "note"), Err(_) => panic!("known valid draft") }\n}',
    'Publishing consumes the draft. A failed transition returns the draft so the caller can repair it; success produces a distinct published value. Private constructors and module boundaries are needed to enforce this design as a public API.',
    'A client connection moves through configured, connected, and authenticated stages with different available operations.',
    'Encoding every runtime possibility into types can make APIs unwieldy; use it where invalid transitions are worth preventing statically.',
    'When would a runtime enum be easier to use than separate state types?',
    'ch18-03-oo-design-patterns.html')
